-- aka.rofl -- AKA: bridges between perspectives.
--
-- Two ontologies over one stream of money. Billing knows ACCOUNTS and the
-- LEGAL ENTITIES it invoices. The CRM knows CUSTOMERS. Neither is a
-- renaming of the other:
--
--   * one customer has several accounts   (northwind: acct_nw_prod, acct_nw_eu)
--   * one account covers several entities (acct_wingtip, a reseller)
--   * some accounts are in no CRM at all  (acct_ghost_ops, acct_orphan_eu)
--   * some customers have no account      (litware)
--
-- The mapping between them is not data either system holds. It is a set of
-- ASSERTIONS BY PEOPLE, and this file models them as such:
--
--   1. A bridge lives in its AUTHOR'S ledger. `maps[integration](b4, ...)`
--      is what the integration team holds true; `authority(integration,
--      integration_team)` is the whole list of who may write there, and an
--      entry written by anybody else surfaces as `forged[audit]` with no
--      enforcement code. Withdrawing a bridge is likewise an entry in its
--      author's own book: only the author may take their own mapping back.
--   2. Two authors may map the same account DIFFERENTLY and both mappings
--      stand -- `maps[integration](b6, acct_adv_a, adventure, 70)` and
--      `maps[finance](b7, acct_adv_a, contoso, 85)` are different facts
--      because their perspectives differ, exactly as two contradicting
--      claims coexist in examples/sus.
--   3. A bridge carries a CONFIDENCE. It is a number its author chose, not
--      a measurement, and section 8 shows the reconciliation refusing two
--      bridges whose confidences said they were fine.
--
-- Every rule below that reads one book and writes another names its head
-- perspective. That is what makes the kernel emit `bridge_decl(Rule, From,
-- To)` and keeps `leak[audit]` empty -- and it is a DIFFERENT bridge from
-- the ones above: the kernel's licenses a rule to read a ledger, an entry
-- in `maps` licenses one entity to stand for another. See README.md.
--
-- Data is synthetic and hand-built. Amounts are whole dollars, confidences
-- whole percent: the kernel's arithmetic is integer-only.

-- =========================================================================
-- 0. who may write where
-- =========================================================================
-- Three source ledgers of record ([billing], [crm], and [main] for the
-- parameters of the run), three ledgers of MAPPINGS -- one per author -- and
-- one derived ledger, [recon], the book of the reconciliation discipline,
-- whose only writers are the rules in sections 3 to 9.

authority(main, analyst).
authority(billing, billing_export).
authority(crm, crm_export).
authority(integration, integration_team).
authority(finance, finance_ops).
authority(sales, sales_ops).

-- A bridge its author has taken back. Host-supplied and EMPTY in the
-- shipped model -- demo.ts loads one in section 7 -- so it is declared
-- extensional here, or `undefined_premise[audit]` reads that emptiness as a
-- misspelt relation. Declaring an input is the remedy, not a waiver.
edb(withdrawn).

-- The quarter under reconciliation. Swapping this fact and evaluating once
-- is the whole of "as of another quarter": see demo.ts `asOf`.
quarter[main](q3).

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. the billing book -- [billing], written by the billing export
-- =========================================================================
-- Billing has never heard of a customer. It has accounts, the legal
-- entities it raises invoices against, and invoices.

-- @who billing_export

account[billing](acct_nw_prod,    active).
account[billing](acct_nw_eu,      active).
account[billing](acct_contoso,    active).
account[billing](acct_wingtip,    active).
account[billing](acct_adv_a,      active).
account[billing](acct_tailspin,   active).
account[billing](acct_ghost_ops,  active).
account[billing](acct_orphan_eu,  active).
account[billing](acct_litware_x,  closed).

-- the legal entities on each account. acct_wingtip has TWO: it is a
-- reseller account, and the invoices on it are raised for both.
entity[billing](acct_nw_prod,   northwind_traders_ltd).
entity[billing](acct_nw_eu,     northwind_traders_bv).
entity[billing](acct_contoso,   contoso_ltd).
entity[billing](acct_wingtip,   fabrikam_inc).
entity[billing](acct_wingtip,   tailspin_toys).
entity[billing](acct_adv_a,     adventure_works_gmbh).
entity[billing](acct_tailspin,  tailspin_toys_llc).
entity[billing](acct_ghost_ops, ghost_operations_ltd).
entity[billing](acct_orphan_eu, orphan_holdings_sarl).
entity[billing](acct_litware_x, litware_x_ag).

-- invoice(Id, Account, Amount, Quarter)
invoice[billing](inv_01, acct_nw_prod,   240000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_02, acct_nw_prod,   180000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_03, acct_nw_eu,     130000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_04, acct_nw_eu,      95000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_05, acct_contoso,   210000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_06, acct_contoso,   155000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_07, acct_tailspin,  135000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_08, acct_wingtip,    60000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_09, acct_wingtip,    45000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_10, acct_adv_a,      60000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_11, acct_ghost_ops,  88000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_12, acct_orphan_eu,  22000, q3).
invoice[billing](inv_13, acct_nw_prod,   200000, q2).
invoice[billing](inv_14, acct_contoso,   150000, q2).
invoice[billing](inv_15, acct_tailspin,   90000, q2).

-- The index exists for the same reason SUS indexes its players: the kernel
-- has no aggregation, so a sum is a SCAN (section 6), and a scan needs a
-- position to step through.
inv_no[billing](inv_01,  1).   inv_no[billing](inv_02,  2).
inv_no[billing](inv_03,  3).   inv_no[billing](inv_04,  4).
inv_no[billing](inv_05,  5).   inv_no[billing](inv_06,  6).
inv_no[billing](inv_07,  7).   inv_no[billing](inv_08,  8).
inv_no[billing](inv_09,  9).   inv_no[billing](inv_10, 10).
inv_no[billing](inv_11, 11).   inv_no[billing](inv_12, 12).
inv_no[billing](inv_13, 13).   inv_no[billing](inv_14, 14).
inv_no[billing](inv_15, 15).
rows[billing](15).

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. the CRM book -- [crm], written by the CRM export
-- =========================================================================
-- The CRM has never heard of an account. It has customers and the revenue
-- its own pipeline booked against them.

-- @who crm_export

customer[crm](northwind, "Northwind Traders").
customer[crm](contoso,   "Contoso Ltd").
customer[crm](fabrikam,  "Fabrikam Inc").
customer[crm](tailspin,  "Tailspin Toys").
customer[crm](wingtip,   "Wingtip Partners").
customer[crm](adventure, "Adventure Works").
customer[crm](litware,   "Litware").

cust_no[crm](northwind, 1).  cust_no[crm](contoso,   2).
cust_no[crm](fabrikam,  3).  cust_no[crm](tailspin,  4).
cust_no[crm](wingtip,   5).  cust_no[crm](adventure, 6).
cust_no[crm](litware,   7).

-- booked(Customer, Amount, Quarter): what the CRM says the quarter was
-- worth for that customer. This is the other system's OWN reported total,
-- and it is the hard number the reconciliation has to close against.
booked[crm](northwind, 645000, q3).
booked[crm](contoso,   365000, q3).
booked[crm](fabrikam,       0, q3).
booked[crm](tailspin,  240000, q3).
booked[crm](wingtip,        0, q3).
booked[crm](adventure,      0, q3).
booked[crm](litware,    88000, q3).
booked[crm](northwind, 200000, q2).
booked[crm](contoso,   150000, q2).
booked[crm](fabrikam,       0, q2).
booked[crm](tailspin,   90000, q2).
booked[crm](wingtip,        0, q2).
booked[crm](adventure,      0, q2).
booked[crm](litware,        0, q2).

-- =========================================================================
-- 3. the bridges -- one ledger per author
-- =========================================================================
-- `maps[X](Id, Account, Customer, Confidence)`: author X asserts that this
-- billing account is that CRM customer, and how sure they are, in percent.
--
-- Nothing here is a join. Every line is somebody's opinion, dated by the
-- ledger it sits in and revocable by its author alone. Three shapes worth
-- naming, all three present below and all three DERIVED in section 5:
--
--   corroborated  two authors, the same target      (acct_contoso)
--   split         one author, two targets           (acct_wingtip, a reseller)
--   disputed      two authors, different targets    (acct_adv_a)

-- @who integration_team

maps[integration](b1, acct_nw_prod,  northwind, 95).
maps[integration](b2, acct_nw_eu,    northwind, 80).
maps[integration](b3, acct_contoso,  contoso,   90).
maps[integration](b4, acct_wingtip,  fabrikam,  60).
maps[integration](b5, acct_wingtip,  tailspin,  55).
maps[integration](b6, acct_adv_a,    adventure, 70).

-- @who finance_ops

maps[finance](b7, acct_adv_a,   contoso, 85).
maps[finance](b8, acct_contoso, contoso, 90).

-- @who sales_ops

maps[sales](b9, acct_tailspin, tailspin, 75).

-- @who analyst

-- =========================================================================
-- 4. the inquiry's index of the bridges -- [recon]
-- =========================================================================
-- Every rule here reads an author's book and writes the reconciliation's.
-- That crossing is the point, and naming the head perspective is what
-- declares it: the kernel emits `bridge_decl(R, integration, recon)` for
-- the first of these, and `leak[audit]` stays empty because of it.
--
-- Written out one rule per author rather than once with a variable
-- perspective, for the reason examples/sus records: `maps[X](..)` reduces
-- to `$any` in the audit, and a bridge declared from `$any` licenses
-- reading EVERY ledger at once, which is not what is meant here.

bridge[recon](B, integration, A, C, Conf) :- maps[integration](B, A, C, Conf).
bridge[recon](B, finance,     A, C, Conf) :- maps[finance](B, A, C, Conf).
bridge[recon](B, sales,       A, C, Conf) :- maps[sales](B, A, C, Conf).

-- A withdrawal is an entry in the withdrawing author's OWN book. Nobody
-- can retract somebody else's mapping without it surfacing as forgery.
retracted[recon](B) :- withdrawn[integration](B).
retracted[recon](B) :- withdrawn[finance](B).
retracted[recon](B) :- withdrawn[sales](B).

-- =========================================================================
-- 5. what a bridge set says about one account
-- =========================================================================

target[recon](A, C)         :- bridge[recon](_, _, A, C, _).
mapped_acct[recon](A)       :- bridge[recon](_, _, A, _, _).
unmapped_acct[recon](A)     :- account[billing](A, _), not mapped_acct[recon](A).

-- two authors, the same target: the mapping is CORROBORATED, and the count
-- of derivations of `target` is how many authors say it (section 9).
corroborated[recon](A, C)   :- bridge[recon](_, X1, A, C, _), bridge[recon](_, X2, A, C, _), X1 != X2.
-- one author, two targets: the account genuinely covers both, and the
-- bridge vocabulary cannot say in what proportion.
split[recon](A, C1, C2)     :- bridge[recon](_, X, A, C1, _), bridge[recon](_, X, A, C2, _),
                               cust_no[crm](C1, I1), cust_no[crm](C2, I2), I1 < I2.
-- two authors, different targets: a DISPUTE. Both entries stand.
disputed[recon](A, C1, C2)  :- bridge[recon](_, X1, A, C1, _), bridge[recon](_, X2, A, C2, _),
                               X1 != X2, cust_no[crm](C1, I1), cust_no[crm](C2, I2), I1 < I2.

-- =========================================================================
-- 6. carrying the money across, and refusing to do it silently
-- =========================================================================
-- `link` is STRUCTURAL and survives a withdrawal, for the reason SUS builds
-- its support graph on `clash` rather than on `conflict`: the question in
-- section 7 is what a total was BUILT on, and retracting a bridge must not
-- quietly erase the fact that the total ever rested on it.

link[recon](Inv, C, B)      :- invoice[billing](Inv, A, _, Q), quarter[main](Q),
                               bridge[recon](B, _, A, C, _).
live_link[recon](Inv, C, B) :- link[recon](Inv, C, B), not retracted[recon](B).
route[recon](Inv, C)        :- live_link[recon](Inv, C, _).
-- one derivation per live mapping path. Counted in section 9; more than one
-- is a defect ONLY when the paths land on different customers, which is what
-- `ambiguous` says and the number alone does not.
paths[recon](Inv)           :- live_link[recon](Inv, _, _).

carried[recon](Inv)         :- route[recon](Inv, _).
ambiguous[recon](Inv)       :- route[recon](Inv, C1), route[recon](Inv, C2), C1 != C2.
in_quarter[recon](Inv)      :- invoice[billing](Inv, _, _, Q), quarter[main](Q).
unmapped[recon](Inv)        :- in_quarter[recon](Inv), not carried[recon](Inv).
-- THE REFUSAL. An ambiguous invoice is not attributed to anybody. It is not
-- resolved by picking the confident candidate, because the confidence is
-- its author's opinion and section 8 shows the money disagreeing with it.
attributed[recon](Inv, C)   :- route[recon](Inv, C), not ambiguous[recon](Inv).
loose[recon](Inv)           :- ambiguous[recon](Inv).
loose[recon](Inv)           :- unmapped[recon](Inv).

-- =========================================================================
-- 7. the totals -- a sum is a scan
-- =========================================================================
-- The kernel has no aggregation, and this is what that costs: to add up a
-- set of invoices you index them and walk the index, carrying the running
-- total, skipping the positions that are not in the pot. One chain serves
-- every pot at once, because the pot is a term.
--
--   billing        every invoice in the quarter
--   attributed     every invoice that landed on exactly one customer
--   ambiguous      money with more than one candidate customer
--   unmapped       money with none
--   cust(C)        what billing says C is worth, through the bridges
--   loose(A)       account A's money that landed nowhere -- section 8 reads it
--   crm_booked     what the CRM says the quarter is worth, over its own index

tag[recon](billing)     :- quarter[main](_).
tag[recon](attributed)  :- quarter[main](_).
tag[recon](ambiguous)   :- quarter[main](_).
tag[recon](unmapped)    :- quarter[main](_).
tag[recon](crm_booked)  :- quarter[main](_).
tag[recon](cust(C))     :- customer[crm](C, _).
tag[recon](loose(A))    :- account[billing](A, _).

-- what each pot counts, said once, so that section 9 can ask a pot which
-- invoices it took and not have to be told a second time.
counts[recon](billing, Inv)    :- in_quarter[recon](Inv).
counts[recon](attributed, Inv) :- attributed[recon](Inv, _).
counts[recon](ambiguous, Inv)  :- ambiguous[recon](Inv).
counts[recon](unmapped, Inv)   :- unmapped[recon](Inv).
counts[recon](cust(C), Inv)    :- attributed[recon](Inv, C).
counts[recon](loose(A), Inv)   :- loose[recon](Inv), invoice[billing](Inv, A, _, _).

tagged[recon](T, N, Amt)          :- counts[recon](T, Inv), inv_no[billing](Inv, N),
                                     invoice[billing](Inv, _, Amt, _).
-- the CRM's own total walks the CUSTOMER index instead; the two index
-- spaces never meet, because a tag never mixes them.
tagged[recon](crm_booked, I, Amt) :- booked[crm](C, Amt, Q), quarter[main](Q), cust_no[crm](C, I).

at[recon](T, N)    :- tagged[recon](T, N, _).
scan[recon](T, 0, 0) :- tag[recon](T).
scan[recon](T, N, S) :- scan[recon](T, M, S0), N is M + 1, tagged[recon](T, N, Amt), S is S0 + Amt.
scan[recon](T, N, S) :- scan[recon](T, M, S), N is M + 1, rows[billing](L), N <= L,
                        not at[recon](T, N).
total[recon](T, S)   :- scan[recon](T, L, S), rows[billing](L).

-- =========================================================================
-- 8. does it close, and if not, what mapping would close it
-- =========================================================================

gap[recon](C, G)     :- total[recon](cust(C), A), booked[crm](C, B, Q), quarter[main](Q),
                        G is A - B.
reconciles[recon](C) :- gap[recon](C, 0).
short[recon](C, N)   :- gap[recon](C, G), G < 0, N is 0 - G.
over[recon](C, N)    :- gap[recon](C, G), G > 0, N is G.

-- WHYNOT, AS A LIST OF MISSING BRIDGES. An account whose loose money is
-- exactly what a customer is short: a bridge from it to that customer would
-- close the reconciliation, and the engine names which.
would_close[recon](C, A) :- short[recon](C, N), total[recon](loose(A), N), N > 0.
proposed[recon](A)       :- would_close[recon](_, A).
-- and whether the bridge has to be INVENTED or only SHARPENED: if a bridge
-- to that customer already exists, the account is ambiguous rather than
-- unmapped, and the repair is to settle the ambiguity, not to add a line.
sharpen[recon](C, A, B)  :- would_close[recon](C, A), bridge[recon](B, _, A, C, _).
add_bridge[recon](C, A)  :- would_close[recon](C, A), not mapped_acct[recon](A).

-- What no bridge can absorb. An account still holding loose money that no
-- customer's shortfall accounts for -- this is the irreducible residual,
-- and section 10 checks that it equals the two systems' headline difference.
residual[recon](A, S)    :- total[recon](loose(A), S), S > 0, not proposed[recon](A).

-- THE RECONCILIATION REFUTING A BRIDGE. A candidate whose target already
-- closes exactly cannot take the money without breaking a total that
-- currently balances -- so the bridge is wrong however confident its author
-- was. This is evidence overruling an assertion, in one rule.
refuted_bridge[recon](B, C) :- bridge[recon](B, _, A, C, _), residual[recon](A, _),
                               reconciles[recon](C).

-- =========================================================================
-- 9. the mark: this number crossed a perspective boundary
-- =========================================================================
-- A conclusion reached through a bridge must not look like one derived
-- inside a single ontology. `crossed` names the bridge, its author and its
-- confidence, and `within_one_book` is its complement -- `total(billing)`
-- is derived from the billing ledger alone and is marked as such.

-- ONE RULE PER CONCLUSION SHAPE, and that is the honest cost of the mark:
-- it is a modelling obligation, not something the kernel derives. The first
-- draft of this file marked `total(cust(C))` and `gap(C)` and forgot
-- `total(attributed)` and `total(ambiguous)`, which cross bridges just as
-- hard -- and the Viterbi fold in demo.ts §5 caught both, because a fold
-- follows the support graph and cannot be forgotten. The test pins the two
-- against each other: a total is marked as crossed exactly when its Viterbi
-- certainty is below 1.
conclusion[recon](total(T))   :- total[recon](T, _).
conclusion[recon](gap(C))     :- gap[recon](C, _).
crossed[recon](total(cust(C)), B, X, Conf)    :- attributed[recon](Inv, C),
                                                 live_link[recon](Inv, C, B),
                                                 bridge[recon](B, X, _, _, Conf).
crossed[recon](total(attributed), B, X, Conf) :- attributed[recon](Inv, C),
                                                 live_link[recon](Inv, C, B),
                                                 bridge[recon](B, X, _, _, Conf).
crossed[recon](total(ambiguous), B, X, Conf)  :- ambiguous[recon](Inv),
                                                 live_link[recon](Inv, _, B),
                                                 bridge[recon](B, X, _, _, Conf).
crossed[recon](gap(C), B, X, Conf)            :- gap[recon](C, _),
                                                 crossed[recon](total(cust(C)), B, X, Conf).
crossing[recon](X)            :- crossed[recon](X, _, _, _).
-- `total(billing)`, `total(crm_booked)` and `total(unmapped)` land here.
-- The first two belong here. THE THIRD IS THE TRAP: it rests on the ABSENCE
-- of a bridge, and an absence carries no provenance, so no mark can reach
-- the number in this whole report that is most sensitive to the bridge set.
within_one_book[recon](X)     :- conclusion[recon](X), not crossing[recon](X).

-- =========================================================================
-- 10. what a total was built on, and what a withdrawal takes with it
-- =========================================================================
-- The support graph is built on `link`, never on `live_link`: retracting a
-- bridge must mark what rested on it, not erase the fact that it did. The
-- machinery is examples/oops's, applied to bridges instead of to papers.

-- The attribution as it would stand if no bridge had been taken back. This
-- is the skeleton the support graph is drawn on: `attributed` collapses
-- when a bridge is retracted, and a collapsed support cannot be marked --
-- it has already vanished, taking with it the record that the total ever
-- rested on it. Measured: without this pair of rules the withdrawal in
-- section 7 leaves `shaken` empty, because northwind's total no longer
-- admits that it was ever built on the two invoices it just lost.
declared_amb[recon](Inv)     :- link[recon](Inv, C1, _), link[recon](Inv, C2, _), C1 != C2.
declared_attr[recon](Inv, C) :- link[recon](Inv, C, _), not declared_amb[recon](Inv).

rests_on[recon](attribution(Inv, C), B)              :- link[recon](Inv, C, B).
rests_on[recon](total(cust(C)), attribution(Inv, C)) :- declared_attr[recon](Inv, C).
rests_on[recon](reconciliation(C), total(cust(C)))   :- customer[crm](C, _).
rests_on[recon](X, Z)                                :- rests_on[recon](X, Y), rests_on[recon](Y, Z).

leaf[recon](X, B)  :- rests_on[recon](X, B), bridge[recon](B, _, _, _, _).
gone[recon](X)     :- leaf[recon](X, B), retracted[recon](B).
standing[recon](X) :- leaf[recon](X, B), not retracted[recon](B).
at_risk[recon](X)  :- gone[recon](X), not standing[recon](X).
shaken[recon](X)   :- gone[recon](X), standing[recon](X).

-- Acceptance scenario (examples/aka/demo.ts runs all of it):
--   aka trace revenue_q3 --from billing --to crm  -> 1.42M / 1.31M / 0.11M
--   ? ambiguous[recon](Inv)                       -> inv_08, inv_09, inv_10
--   ? disputed[recon](acct_adv_a, C1, C2)         -> adventure vs contoso
--   aka -n tailspin                               -> sharpen b5; aka -n litware -> add a bridge
--   withdraw b2                                   -> at_risk / shaken reach the reconciliation
--   ? refuted_bridge[recon](B, C)                 -> b6 and b7, both of them

-- ============ what [recon] is allowed to read ============
--
-- Written out because the kernel stopped writing it. `crossing` carried
-- `not bridge_decl(R, A, B)`, emitted for any rule whose head named a ledger
-- and whose body read another, so every rule in this file licensed its own
-- read by existing and `leak[audit]` was 0 for a reason unrelated to the file.
-- Six crossings report now, and every one of them is [recon] reading a book
-- it reconciles.
--
-- That is what a reconciliation ledger IS: [recon] holds the conclusions of
-- comparing the export books against each other, so it must read all of them.
-- Six separate sentences and not one blanket, because `imports` names the
-- pair — a seventh export book added tomorrow gets a seventh line, and until
-- it does the audit says no about it.
imports(recon, billing).
imports(recon, crm).
imports(recon, finance).
imports(recon, integration).
imports(recon, sales).

-- and [main], where the analyst's own working facts live.
imports(recon, main).
